> On Mar 14, 2:59 pm, Derek Ray <de...@moot.its.only.a.spamtrap.org>
>> On 2008-03-14, money cat <mnard...@gmail.com> wrote:
>> > 4) You are playing a Tourist and have the Platinum Yendorian Express
>> > Card.
>> So how does this work then for the other 11 roles?  (A Wizard can use
>> the spell effectively instead by just finding or writing the book.)
>
> Without an efficient source of charging, the overall benefit to the
> character is significantly diminished.  What remains unchanged,
> however, is the ratio of useful items per wand : per marker.

Except the ratio isn't meaningful unless you can iterate over the random
chance enough times to begin approaching the average.

A 70% chance of winning on a die roll is nothing I'd bet my house on 
unless we got to roll the die 1,000 times.

>> So the question then becomes -- does ease-of-access to this make the
>> Tourist and Wizard too easy?
>
> Perhaps.  The Tourist quest is already overloaded with stuff; to add
> an infinite source of blessed charging seems gratuitous.  Perhaps some
> limited number of charges (6-8) on the PYEC would be a solution here.

The PYEC appears to have been added for 'theme' and for the obvious pun
on its name... but not so much WRT balance.  It's been adjusted
downwards at least once, but there's always the possibility that the
mechanic itself could need revisiting.

> With respect to Wizards, I'm not sure... maybe the "polymorph" spell
> should be replaced with "polymorph creature."

Chopping down polymorph too sharply is pretty rough.

> Another, more drastic option would be to eliminate wands of polymorph
> altogether and increase the frequency of !oPoly.

Yeah.  See, a certain amount of polypiling is one of the "fun" things
about Nethack -- if you're clever, you wedge a bunch of stuff up and get
a whole bunch of new stuff, totally at random.  Eliminating the wand
would be a little extreme, I think.

Upping the chance of golems (and making golems actually dangerous) might
not be so bad, though.  Big rewards should generally come with big
risks.  (Adding golems that could only be obtained from polymorph
attempts might be even better.)

